## Notes: Prettier chat messages
## Author: aDd1kTeD2Ka0s
## Version: 1.5.0
## IconTexture: Interface\AddOns\PrettyChat\media\logos\prettychat.logo.128.tga
## SavedVariables: PrettyChatDB
## OptionalDeps: Ace3, LibStub, CallbackHandler-1.0, LibDataBroker-1.1, LibDBIcon-1.0
## DefaultState: enabled
## Category-enUS: Chat & Communication
## X-License: MIT
## X-Standard: https://github.com/tusharsaxena/WowAddonStandards
## X-Curse-Project-ID: 919766

# Libraries (must load first)
libs\LibStub\LibStub.lua
libs\CallbackHandler-1.0\CallbackHandler-1.0.xml
libs\AceAddon-3.0\AceAddon-3.0.xml
libs\AceDB-3.0\AceDB-3.0.xml
libs\AceConsole-3.0\AceConsole-3.0.xml
libs\AceGUI-3.0\AceGUI-3.0.xml
# The broker pair, and they are the LAUNCHER's (launcher-§1), not LibKa0s's. The one
# LibDataBroker object core\LauncherSetup.lua builds is handed to LibDBIcon, which draws the
# minimap button from it; a broker display draws its own row from the same object. LibKa0s
# resolves both with LibStub(..., true) at Register time and degrades by name, so the order
# within this block is not load-bearing -- they are listed before LibKa0s.xml for readability.
libs\LibDataBroker-1.1\LibDataBroker-1.1.lua
libs\LibDBIcon-1.0\LibDBIcon-1.0.lua
libs\LibKa0s\LibKa0s.xml

# Locales (locale table — no earlier-load dependency; toc-file-§5 section order)
locales\enUS.lua

# Core (the LibKa0s seams load first). Constants, Namespace, State, Database and PrettyChat are
# in conventional order, and each load-bearing line carries its own note directly above it.
# core\EnvSetup.lua loads BEFORE core\Namespace.lua and the position is load-bearing:
# Namespace, settings\Panel.lua and settings\Slash.lua ALL read the TOC at FILE SCOPE, so the
# seam has to exist before them. Each of those reads resolves once, at load, and keeps the
# answer for the whole session. They call the seam UNGUARDED, deliberately: a seam published
# later raises here and now rather than leaving a version silently pinned to a literal.
core\EnvSetup.lua
# core\MediaSetup.lua loads BEFORE core\Constants.lua and the position is load-bearing:
# Constants resolves FONT_MONO through NS.MediaFont at load, so the seam must be published first.
core\MediaSetup.lua
core\Constants.lua
core\Namespace.lua
core\State.lua
# core\Util.lua loads AFTER core\Constants.lua and the position is load-bearing: Util takes
# NS.Const.Color — which core\Constants.lua publishes — as a file-scope upvalue, so a Util
# that loaded first would index a nil NS.Const and raise before the addon ever runs.
core\Util.lua
core\Database.lua
core\PrettyChat.lua
# core\CoreSetup.lua loads AFTER core\PrettyChat.lua and the position is load-bearing: NewAddon's
# AceConsole embed overwrites NS.Print, and CoreSetup's file-scope `NS.Print = printer.Print`
# reclaims it (anti-pattern #36). Moved earlier, nothing errors, but every chat line turns
# AceConsole-green.
core\CoreSetup.lua
# core\DebugLogSetup.lua loads AFTER core\Constants.lua and the position is load-bearing: its
# file-scope lib:New descriptor reads NS.Const.FONT_MONO. NS.State and NS.Print are read at call
# time, so they do not pin it.
core\DebugLogSetup.lua
# core\LifecycleSetup.lua loads AFTER core\DebugLogSetup.lua (the NS.Debug seam the two
# stand-down arms trace through) and BEFORE core\LauncherSetup.lua. It publishes NS.Lifecycle,
# the one latch this addon is stood down by (slash-commands-§7); modules\Override.lua's show
# ladder and settings\Schema.lua's Enable row both read it. Nothing resolves at load beyond the
# LibStub lookup -- both arms reach PrettyChat through closures at CALL time -- so the position
# is conventional rather than load-bearing.
core\LifecycleSetup.lua
# core\LauncherSetup.lua loads AFTER core\PrettyChat.lua (the AceAddon object its
# openSettings hook calls OpenConfig on) and after core\DebugLogSetup.lua (the NS.Debug seam it
# hands over as `debug`). Nothing resolves at load beyond the LibStub lookup, so unlike
# core\MediaSetup.lua above the position is conventional rather than load-bearing: the object is
# built here and REGISTERED from OnEnable, because `minimap` cannot answer until AceDB has run.
core\LauncherSetup.lua

# Defaults (data tables — must precede settings\Schema.lua)
defaults\Profile.lua
defaults\Defaults.lua

# Modules (the override pipeline). Conventional: modules\Override.lua reads NS.Defaults, NS.Util
# and the AceAddon object, all published above.
modules\Override.lua
# modules\Diagnostics.lua loads AFTER modules\Override.lua and conventionally: every NS member its
# sections read (NS.Schema, NS.Helpers, NS.Launcher, the Override engine) is read when a report RUNS,
# and core\DebugLogSetup.lua asks for the sections then too, so nothing here resolves at load.
modules\Diagnostics.lua

# Settings. The annotated lines (OptionsSetup, Slash, Panel) are load-bearing; the rest of the
# order is conventional.
settings\Schema.lua
# settings\OptionsSetup.lua loads AFTER settings\Schema.lua and the position is load-bearing: its
# file-scope descriptor takes NS.SchemaRuntime.Get/Set/ApplyDefault/AllRows as values, and it calls
# NS.Schema.InstallMasterControls at file scope.
settings\OptionsSetup.lua
# settings\Slash.lua loads AFTER settings\Schema.lua and the position is load-bearing: the
# library-path descriptor takes NS.SchemaRuntime.Get/Set/FindRow/AllRows/ApplyDefault as values at
# file load.
settings\Slash.lua
# settings\Panel.lua loads AFTER settings\OptionsSetup.lua and settings\Schema.lua, and the
# position is load-bearing: Panel takes NS.Helpers — which settings\OptionsSetup.lua publishes
# — and NS.Schema as file-scope upvalues, and then CALLS Helpers.RegisterOptionsPage at file
# scope for both of its pages, so both seams have to resolve before this line is reached.
settings\Panel.lua
